{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head f81a102d1aad273b704fc435afaae02e00a841c3 for request #9796, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d29c09faef7e54cdf1199eb86344fa7062d38177. All 19 artifacts inspected, with plans/README.md reporting artifact first. One P2 finding remains.\n\n[P2] Make existing operational error fields safe before exposing them after upgrade. plans/README.md@f81a102d1aad273b704fc435afaae02e00a841c3:651 explicitly leaves prior rows unchanged. That limits the fix to new writes and does not meet the request's “every durable or projected error field” condition or existing R-WS-1/R-WS-4. LandingCore.status returns the stored lastError verbatim (packages/git/src/landing/core.ts@f81a102d1aad273b704fc435afaae02e00a841c3:293); Workspaces.view parses and returns the stored error.message verbatim (packages/git/src/workspace/workspaces.ts@f81a102d1aad273b704fc435afaae02e00a841c3:316). After reopening a publication record with the previous engine's main-read error form, PublicationStatus still contains the echoed bearer credential. After reopening a failed workspace with its previous error form, WorkspaceOp still contains that credential. No remote call or subsequent retry is needed. These are two independently executed named assertion failures at this exact head, not an inference from test coverage. Ending a row does not necessarily erase it: workspace revoke preserves its error, and terminal landing bodies remain stored.\n\nRepair the mutable operational fields rather than waiting for another failure to overwrite them. Cover existing landing lastError and push detail, workspace error/step errors, snapshot duty errors, job-token errors and publication_error according to which can contain the previous provider text. Use safe replacement metadata for legacy text; a regex cannot establish that arbitrary old text is safe. Make current projections safe before a cleanup pass can expose a pending legacy row. Preserve ownership, token IDs, expiry/deadline checks, unknown-versus-known effects, held job-token state, cleanup obligations, retry timing and signed history. Keep the upgrade work bounded and idempotent, with no repeated idle writes after it finishes. Add stored-room/reopen controls, including completed/terminal rows, and mutate the new upgrade/read guards. Update the no-migration choice and the evidence at one repaired exact head on the same request.\n\nArchitecture: the affected Artroom components are the Git landing engine and publishers, workspace/snapshot services and Room job/publication orchestration, plus their operation and operator projections. This is ordinary application code coordinated through Workroom. Relative to Gitseq's docs/reference/architecture.md, it changes none of storage/kernel, host binding, application-profile authority, projection authority or CLI/MCP layer contracts. Artroom's existing no-credential contract in docs/protocol.md applies; no new normative authority or protocol change is intended. The implementation retains error fields' string shape and existing control flow, and expands the existing errorNote helper rather than introducing another service. The missing stored-state coverage is a security defect, not a justification to weaken that contract.\n\nSecurity: the new-write paths are materially improved. Both standard publishers and the engine catch supply errorNote for integration; readiness, lost pushes and failed main reads use it; answered push details keep fixed outcomes/refusal metadata; workspace, snapshot and job error writes stop retaining pattern-redacted provider messages. knownArtifactsCode/publicationCode filter publication codes before storage and the caller's message. I examined the fixed names/codes and bounded numeric/status values, the provider-to-sandbox-to-Room path, operator diagnosis redaction, signed member/configuration text and the other sinks listed in the report. The changed job-token test strengthens exact-metadata and all-row assertions. Choices 1, 3, 4 and 5 are consistent with the inspected standard implementations; choice 2 is rejected by the finding above. No additional concrete new-write defect was established.\n\nThe inherited mint authority's four facts remain established: planner held ratifier authority through the effective operator foundation #1; request #8651 explicitly commissioned the contract decision and its separate implementation lanes; independent approval #9463 and sealed landing #9465 satisfied that decision request; its merged note #9467 and governing chain are effective, current and unretired before follow-on work. Fresh durable inspection, the role projection at #9897 and its non-stale merged-note pointer confirm this. The provider-error request and builder promise #9876 are effective; this review conveys no live credential or deployment authority.\n\nSimplification and breadth: retaining one shared errorNote and one small outcomeNote avoids scattered regex rules while keeping useful stage/code/status diagnostics for people and agents. Keep the existing retry and ownership machinery. A single documented legacy-field policy with bounded upgrade coverage is simpler and safer than leaving each reader to distinguish old unsafe strings indefinitely. Do not settle or discard cleanup debt to remove an error. Full mint lane C, cleanup visibility, complete cost accounting and the broader platform scope remain open; schedule estimates do not narrow them.\n\nValidation, independently run and read at f81a102d: npm ci exit 0; root npm run typecheck exit 0; root npm test exit 0 (2,268 passed, one skipped), including Git Node 297, Room Node 197 and Room Workers 472; separate Git test:workers exit 0 (11 passed). In a separate exact-head guard clone, independently replayed M1-M21: 21 named runtime-red outcomes, no transform/load failures or surviving mutations. After restoration, the 12 d29c09fa Node tests and both Room files (43 tests) passed, exit 0; the guard checkout is tracked-clean. The initial M16 runner path was a module-load failure and was not counted; it was corrected, then M16-M21 executed assertions. The two independent legacy-reopen controls each failed their named security assertion (process exit 1). git diff --check passed; pristine review and canonical tracked trees remain clean, canonical main still 574568b2. No live provider action, deployment, canonical source edit or merge was performed.\n\nThis verdict leaves the implementation open and authorizes no merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:24eab9f4870ec2a9ea5b1131a7d5e8ad5e487f7f","binding":"assigned","head":"f81a102d1aad273b704fc435afaae02e00a841c3","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c095db4a631d81e09a7259f78a23bc379c1810f1\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:60d5c3a62d4ea5f8bea1cdb923381a13b10d1d0f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c095db4a631d81e09a7259f78a23bc379c1810f1","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}